      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest number \\( n \\) that satisfies the following conditions:\n1. \\( n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\)\n2. \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\)\n3. \\( n > 10 \\)\n\nLet's start by expressing these congruences mathematically:\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 \\]\n\\[ n = 5m + 2 \\]\n\nWe need to find a common \\( n \\) that satisfies both equations. Substituting the first equation into the second equation, we get:\n\\[ 4k + 3 = 5m + 2 \\]\n\\[ 4k - 5m = -1 \\]\n\\[ 4k = 5m - 1 \\]\n\\[ k = \\frac{5m - 1}{4} \\]\n\nFor \\( k \\) to be an integer, \\( 5m - 1 \\) must be divisible by 4. We can check the smallest values of \\( m \\) to find a suitable \\( n \\):\n\n- If \\( m = 1 \\), then \\( 5m - 1 = 4 \\), which is divisible by 4. So, \\( k = 1 \\).\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 = 4(1) + 3 = 7 \\]\nSince \\( n = 7 \\) is not greater than 10, we try the next value of \\( m \\):\n- If \\( m = 2 \\), then \\( 5m - 1 = 9 \\), which is not divisible by 4.\n- If \\( m = 3 \\), then \\( 5m - 1 = 14 \\), which is not divisible by 4.\n- If \\( m = 4 \\), then \\( 5m - 1 = 19 \\), which is not divisible by 4.\n- If \\( m = 5 \\), then \\( 5m - 1 = 24 \\), which is divisible by 4. So, \\( k = 6 \\).\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 = 4(6) + 3 = 27 \\]\n\nSince \\( n = 27 \\) is greater than 10, we check if it satisfies both original congruences:\n1. \\( 27 \\div 4 = 6 \\) remainder \\( 3 \\), so \\( 27 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\).\n2. \\( 27 \\div 5 = 5 \\) remainder \\( 2 \\), so \\( 27 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\).\n\nBoth conditions are satisfied, so the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{27} \\]",
